The residual light-dependent signal with the second (benzyl-alcohol) probe cannot arise from phenoxyl-radical chemistry (B), which requires the phenolic OH that is absent here. It also cannot be explained by the sensitizer acting alone in a probe-independent manner (A), because that would not account for the fact that the observable difference is strictly probe-dependent and tied to the shared bicyclo[4.2.0]octadiene core. Carbene formation (D) has no mechanistic basis under these conditions, and Cy5-azide (E) is added after irradiation.

The only species that remains chemically plausible is therefore the electrophilic Michael acceptor generated by sensitized [2+2] cycloreversion of that common core (C). This fragmentation product is formed (albeit inefficiently) from either probe when the thioxanthenone is excited at 417 nm, furnishing the weaker but still detectable light-vs-dark difference observed on SDS-PAGE after CuAAC. This matches the experimental design exactly: identical core + identical sensitizer + identical readout, with only the phenol-dependent radical pathway removed.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

9.text:
The second probe lacks the phenol, eliminating efficient phenoxyl-radical formation (B) and any associated strong labeling. The residual light-dependent signal (weaker but still observable vs. dark control) is therefore produced by the only component that both (a) absorbs 417 nm light and (b) remains present and unchanged: the thioxanthenone photosensitizer itself. This species can drive weaker background pathways such as direct H-atom abstraction or Type-II (ROS) chemistry on the benzyl-alcohol probe or on endogenous protein residues, ultimately yielding the observed Cy5 signal after click chemistry.

C is chemically implausible here: the bicyclo[4.2.0] core is common to both probes, yet the residual labeling intensity drops sharply when the phenol is removed; sensitized visible-light cycloreversion to the Michael acceptor is not expected to be efficient under the stated conditions. D and E are formed (or applied) after the light step and cannot explain a light-dependent difference. Thus the molecule responsible is A.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. 2-fluoro-7-methoxy-9H-thioxanthen-9-one}

